# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WeVolunteer — SERVER runtime environment (template)
#
#   sudo install -d -m 750 -o root -g wev /etc/wevolunteer
#   sudo install -m 640 -o root -g wev deploy/wevolunteer.env.example \
#        /etc/wevolunteer/wevolunteer.env
#   sudo nano /etc/wevolunteer/wevolunteer.env      # fill in every REPLACE_ME
#
# Read by systemd via `EnvironmentFile=` in deploy/wevolunteer.service.
#
# ── What production REFUSES to start without ────────────────────────────────
#
# Verified by booting `node server/server.js` with NODE_ENV=production and
# adding one setting at a time. The server exits with a FATAL message naming the
# missing item, in this order:
#
#   1. JWT_SECRET           — and it must be strong. A placeholder from this
#                             template, anything under 32 characters, or a value
#                             with fewer than 8 distinct characters is refused.
#   2. TFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY   — 32 bytes of base64. The published development key
#                             (`AAAA…=`, which decodes to 32 ZERO bytes) and any
#                             other low-entropy value are refused. It was once
#                             accepted, because only length and base64 validity
#                             were checked.
#   3. SMTP_HOST / SMTP_USER / SMTP_PASS
#   4. APP_URL              — the base URL for password-reset and verification
#                             links.
#
# With all four supplied the server starts and serves the API. Nothing else is a
# boot blocker; everything below is optional or has a working default.
#
# Generate the two secrets INDEPENDENTLY, on the host, and never reuse one for
# both:
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64'))"   # JWT_SECRET
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"   # TFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY
#
#
# ── This file is NOT the frontend config ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# There are two environment files and they must never be merged:
#
#   /etc/wevolunteer/wevolunteer.env   THIS FILE — server secrets, read at
#                                      runtime by systemd. Never in the repo.
#
#   /srv/wevolunteer/.env.production   Frontend build vars (VITE_*) only, read
#                                      by `npm run build`. See
#                                      .env.production.example in the repo root.
#
# They live at different paths precisely so one cannot overwrite the other.
# Putting VITE_* here does nothing — the frontend is compiled before this file
# is ever read. Putting secrets in the other file is pointless and risky.
#
# ── Format notes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# systemd parses this file itself; it is NOT shell. Do not use `export`, and do
# not rely on shell expansion — `$VAR` is not interpolated. A value may contain
# spaces without quoting: everything after the first `=` is taken literally.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ── Required. The server refuses to start without these. ─────────────────────

NODE_ENV=production

# Signing key for session tokens. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# Anyone holding this can mint a token for any user, including an administrator.
JWT_SECRET=REPLACE_ME

# AES-256 key encrypting stored two-factor secrets, base64, exactly 32 bytes.
# Generate with: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#
# Treat this like a backup key rather than a rotatable secret: there is no
# re-encryption path, so changing or losing it makes every enrolled user's TOTP
# secret undecryptable and each account has to be cleared individually with
# bin/reset-2fa.mjs. Back it up wherever you back up the database.
TFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY=REPLACE_ME

# Whether an administrator must hold a second factor to use their privileges.
#
# Defaults to ON. Leave it unset in production — the server REFUSES TO BOOT if
# it is set to "false" while NODE_ENV=production, because an administrator holds
# every capability on the platform and a password alone is not enough to stand
# behind that.
#
# The only value of setting it at all is "false" on a development or demo
# database whose seeded administrator fixtures have no authenticator enrolled.
# Accepts exactly "true", "false", or nothing; any other value is a boot error
# rather than a silently-ignored typo.
# REQUIRE_ADMIN_2FA=true

# Absolute base URL of this deployment. Used to build the links in
# password-reset and email-verification messages. A wrong value emails live
# single-use tokens to a domain you do not control. No trailing slash.
#
# This is the APPLICATION origin, not the marketing site. Under the domain split
# the apex `wevolunteers.org` serves marketing and the application lives at
# `app.wevolunteers.org` — the host deploy/nginx/wevolunteers.conf answers for —
# so every link this value builds, and every path under /api, belongs to the
# `app.` host. Pointing it at the apex sends password-reset links to a site that
# does not implement them.
APP_URL=https://app.wevolunteers.org

# ── Outbound mail ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Mail leaves through three layers, in order:
#
#   1. Resend      the primary. RESEND_API_KEY below.
#   2. SMTP        the emergency fallback, used only when Resend cannot take a
#                  message. SMTP_HOST / SMTP_USER / SMTP_PASS.
#   3. the outbox  a table in the database. If neither provider took it, the
#                  message is written down and retried until it goes.
#
# Production refuses to boot with NEITHER a Resend key nor SMTP credentials: the
# alternative is the development console mock, which prints password-reset
# tokens to the journal and delivers nothing.
#
# Configuring only one is allowed, and is announced at boot. With Resend alone
# there is no fallback, so a Resend outage means mail waits in the outbox rather
# than going out over a second route.

# Resend API key. Create it at resend.com with SEND permission only.
# This is a SECRET: it belongs in this file (mode 0600, owned by root) and
# nowhere else. Never in the repository, never in a VITE_ variable, never in a
# log line. Rotate it by replacing the value here and restarting.
RESEND_API_KEY=REPLACE_ME

# Signing secret for the Resend delivery webhook, issued by Resend when the
# webhook is created. Without it the endpoint refuses every request — fail
# closed, because an endpoint that accepts unsigned delivery events lets anybody
# on the internet mark a student's address as bounced.
#
# This is a SECRET. Same rules as the API key: this file only, never the repo,
# never a VITE_ variable, never a log line.
#
# The webhook itself is created once, at launch, against
#   https://app.wevolunteers.org/api/webhooks/resend
# — the `app.` host, because that is where /api is served. The apex is the
# marketing site and has no API: a webhook registered there 404s every delivery
# event, and a bounce nobody records is a student whose address is quietly
# broken.
# subscribed to email.sent, email.delivered, email.delivery_delayed,
# email.bounced, email.complained and email.failed. Deliberately NOT
# email.opened or email.clicked — see server/resendWebhook.js.
RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=REPLACE_ME

# How far out of step a webhook timestamp may be before the event is refused.
# Milliseconds; default five minutes. This is the replay window: without it a
# captured request stays valid forever.
# RESEND_WEBHOOK_TOLERANCE_MS=300000

# The emergency fallback relay. Strongly recommended, not required.
SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.net
SMTP_USER=REPLACE_ME
SMTP_PASS=REPLACE_ME

# The domain the platform's own addresses live on: noreply@, support@, admin@,
# legal@, privacy@, security@, platform@, contact@ and the owner address.
#
# Every sender is derived from this one value — server/emailIdentity.js decides
# WHICH address a given message uses, and the rule that all authentication mail
# leaves from noreply@ is enforced there rather than configured here.
#
# Whatever you set must be a domain your email provider has verified and your
# SMTP relay may send as, with SPF and DKIM published for it. An unverified sending domain is
# the single most likely failure on the day you switch over; it is classified as
# a configuration error, so those messages queue rather than being discarded.
MAIL_DOMAIN=wevolunteers.org


# ── Strongly recommended ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── The database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# PRODUCTION IS POSTGRESQL. These three lines are what make that true, and
# leaving them out is not a neutral omission: `server/db/engine.js` resolves
# `DATABASE_ENGINE ?? 'sqlite'`, so an environment file without them boots the
# application on a LOCAL SQLITE FILE and says nothing about it. The service
# starts, /health reports "connected and writable", and every student record
# written from that moment lives on the VM's disk instead of in Supabase —
# where the nightly backup is looking for it.
#
# That is exactly the shape this file used to have. It set WEV_DB_PATH and
# nothing else, while deploy/wevolunteer-backup.service ran backup-postgres.sh:
# the two halves of the deployment disagreed about which database production
# used, and the backup was the only one that would have complained.
#
# Required, all three:
DATABASE_ENGINE=postgres

# The Supavisor pooler, NOT the direct db.<ref>.supabase.co host. Port 5432 is
# session mode and 6543 is transaction mode — see the trade-off recorded in
# docs/operations/horizontal-scaling.md before choosing. Session mode is what
# LISTEN/NOTIFY needs; transaction mode spends far fewer connections.
#
# This value carries the database password. It belongs ONLY in
# /etc/wevolunteer/wevolunteer.env, which is root-owned and 0640 to the `wev`
# group. Never in the repository, never in a shell history, never in a ticket.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

# Verifies the server's certificate. Ships in the repository, so this path is
# relative to /srv/wevolunteer and needs no separate distribution. A rotated
# root fails closed, which is correct — confirm it is current for your region
# before launch rather than during an incident.
DATABASE_SSL_CA=deploy/certs/supabase-root-2021.crt

# WEV_DB_PATH is deliberately NOT set.
#
# It selects nothing while DATABASE_ENGINE=postgres, and setting it invites the
# belief that the SQLite file is the production database — which is how the
# wrong thing gets backed up, restored and trusted. The SQLite path exists for
# local development and for the demo database; see
# docs/operations/sqlite-retirement.md for what is still load-bearing.
# WEV_DB_PATH=/var/lib/wevolunteer/wevolunteer.db

# Browser origins allowed to call the API from another origin. Same-origin
# requests always pass, so the single-host topology works without this — set it
# anyway, it is your only control if the client is ever served elsewhere.
# The application origin, the same host as APP_URL — never the marketing apex,
# which has no reason to call the API and is same-site with it (see csrfGuard.js).
CORS_ORIGINS=https://app.wevolunteers.org

# Number of reverse proxies in front of the app. Required for per-IP rate
# limiting to see the real client. Unset behind a proxy = every user shares one
# bucket. Set without a proxy = clients can forge X-Forwarded-For.
TRUST_PROXY=1

# Interface the HTTP server binds. The DEFAULT IS 0.0.0.0 — i.e. reachable on
# every interface the host has, which bypasses nginx and therefore TLS and HSTS.
# For the single-host topology in the runbook, set loopback and firewall the port
# as well. An earlier revision of the runbook wrongly claimed loopback was
# hardcoded; it never was.
BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1

# Where completed data-export archives are written. Each one is a single student's
# complete personal record, so this must be durable, backed-up storage owned by the
# service user. Defaults to an `exports/` directory beside WEV_DB_PATH, which is
# already covered by the unit's ReadWritePaths. Do NOT point this at /tmp: the unit
# sets PrivateTmp=true, so /tmp is destroyed on every restart while the database
# still promises the file for seven days.
# WEV_EXPORT_DIR=/var/lib/wevolunteer/exports

# ── REQUIRED on this unit, despite looking optional ──────────────────────────
#
# Where uploaded avatars and organization images are written.
#
# The default is <checkout>/public/uploads, which is correct in development and
# is inside the READ-ONLY mount on a deployed host: the service unit sets
# ProtectSystem=strict, so everything outside ReadWritePaths= is read-only, and
# the checkout at /srv/wevolunteer is outside it.
#
# The directory is created when the module is imported, so leaving this unset
# does not break uploads — it stops the service from starting at all. The
# application says so by name rather than dying with EROFS, but the fix is here.
#
# Create it first:
#   sudo install -d -o wev -g wev -m 750 /var/lib/wevolunteer/uploads
WEV_UPLOADS_DIR=/var/lib/wevolunteer/uploads

# 587 = STARTTLS (default), 465 = implicit TLS. Nothing else is special-cased.
SMTP_PORT=587


# ── Optional ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# EMAIL_FROM IS NO LONGER USED. Setting it logs a warning at boot and changes
# nothing.
#
# It used to be the single From header on every outbound message — the login
# code, the suspension notice, the privacy-policy update and the shift reminder
# alike. The sender is now chosen per message from what the message IS, so that
# a reply to an enforcement notice reaches somebody and a reply to a login code
# does not carry a live credential into a shared mailbox.
#
# Set MAIL_DOMAIN instead.

# How hard ONE provider is tried before the fallback takes over, and the pause
# between those attempts. Only a genuinely transient failure repeats; a rate
# limit or a refusal is answered on the first reply.
# MAIL_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3
# MAIL_RETRY_DELAY_MS=500

# Attempts a QUEUED message gets before it is marked failed. Backoff is
# exponential with jitter, capped at one hour, so six attempts spans roughly two
# hours.
# MAIL_QUEUE_MAX_ATTEMPTS=6

# How long a DELIVERED outbox row is kept before it is swept, in milliseconds.
# Long enough to answer "did that verification email go out this morning";
# short enough that the table is not an indefinite record of the email addresses
# of minors. Default is one day.
# MAIL_SENT_RETENTION_MS=86400000

# How long to wait for Resend before treating the attempt as transient and
# moving on. Milliseconds.
# RESEND_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# Cap on password-reset / resend-verification requests per 15 minutes, keyed on the
# TARGET ADDRESS (or the session, for the authenticated resend endpoint) — not on IP.
# A school campus egresses from one public address, so a per-IP cap of 5 meant the
# whole building shared five verification emails per window on signup day.
# How many NEW appeals one person may open about the SAME subject in a rolling
# 30 days. Scoped per (person, subject) rather than per person, so a student
# dealing with separate bans from three different clubs is not spending one
# allowance on all of them — each organization's appeal inbox gets the same
# tolerance independently. Withdrawn and decided appeals still count; the loop
# they guard against is made entirely of decided ones.
# APPEAL_WINDOW_LIMIT=5

# How many problem reports one ACCOUNT may file per hour from /report-issue.
# Keyed on the account rather than the address, because a school leaves through
# a single public IP and a per-IP cap would make one building share one
# allowance. Somebody working out what is broken may legitimately file two or
# three in a row; ten is well past honest use and well short of a useful flood.
# ISSUE_REPORT_LIMIT=10

# Cap on ALL /api requests per 15 minutes, keyed on the SESSION when the request
# carries one and on the address otherwise.
#
# This is the broadest limiter in the application and it used to be keyed on the
# address alone. A school egresses from one public IP and a signed-in session
# makes several requests per screen, so 600 was the whole building's budget:
# a few dozen students browsing at lunchtime exhausted it and the next person to
# open the app was refused. The same failure that had already been found and
# fixed for sign-in (LOGIN_RATE_LIMIT) and for mail (MAIL_RATE_LIMIT).
#
# Keying it on the session does not loosen anything — the number per bucket is
# unchanged, an anonymous caller is still bounded by address, and one account is
# still bounded at the same rate. A building simply stops sharing one bucket.
# The session token is VERIFIED before it names a bucket; a forged or expired
# cookie falls back to the address, or rotating one would buy an unlimited
# budget.
#
# Raise it only if a real population needs more than 600 requests per quarter
# hour EACH. bin/load-test.mjs sets it very high so a load run measures the
# application rather than the limiter.
# API_RATE_LIMIT=600

# How long a periodic sweep holds its leader lease, in milliseconds.
#
# The notification sweep, Auto Select, retention and the search indexer each take
# a lease from the `scheduler_leases` table before running, so a second instance
# cannot run the same whole-table scan concurrently. Nothing changes on a
# single-instance deployment: the sole holder always acquires it.
#
# Must comfortably exceed the longest a sweep can legitimately take, or a slow
# pass loses its own lease mid-run and a second process starts a concurrent one —
# exactly what the lease exists to prevent. Sweeps finish in seconds; ten minutes
# is a very wide margin and still short enough that a hard-killed process does not
# block its replacement for long.
#
# The lease FAILS OPEN: if it cannot be taken, the sweep runs anyway. It is not a
# security boundary, and a database hiccup must not silently stop retention.
# SCHEDULER_LEASE_TTL_MS=600000

# Publish connection-pool counts on /health. Off by default.
#
# /health is unauthenticated and deliberately carries no detail — its failure
# branches return no error text so it cannot become a filesystem oracle. Pool
# counts are not user data, but they do publish how much concurrent traffic the
# deployment is carrying, which an uptime monitor does not need.
#
# Turn it on while investigating saturation. `waiting` is the number that matters
# and the only one nothing else reports: the pool is deliberately small, so the
# failure mode under load is requests queueing for a client, which looks exactly
# like "the database got slower" from outside. Absent on SQLite, which has no pool.
# HEALTH_POOL_STATS=false

# MAIL_RATE_LIMIT=5

# Messages one person may send per minute, on a SLIDING window.
#
# Sliding, not fixed, and the distinction is the point: under a fixed minute,
# twenty sends at 00:59 and twenty at 01:01 are both legal, which is forty
# messages in two seconds. The budget here recovers one slot at a time as sends
# age out, so a burst cannot be doubled across a boundary. A refused send does
# not consume budget, so somebody retrying does not extend their own block.
#
# Shared across instances above half the limit (server/messageThrottle.js). A
# sender below ten a minute is ordinary use and costs no Redis command at all;
# only somebody pushing towards the ceiling is worth coordinating about. With
# Redis unconfigured or unreachable this is exactly a correct per-instance
# throttle, which is what the single-instance deployment has always had.
# MESSAGE_RATE_LIMIT=20

# How strictly the volume limiters are enforced across instances. [coordinated]
#
#   coordinated  Exact within an instance; reconciled through Redis only once a
#                caller passes a quarter of the ceiling. Across N instances the
#                bound is `limit + N x (escalateAt - 1)` rather than `N x limit`.
#                Costs ZERO commands for callers below the threshold, which is
#                nearly everybody — and that is the whole point, because
#                metering every /api request would cost about 6,600 commands per
#                user per month against a free plan that allows 500.
#
#   exact        Every request metered. Globally exact for any number of
#                instances. Costs 3 commands per request, which the Upstash free
#                plan cannot carry — but a Redis or Valkey plan that meters
#                CONNECTIONS rather than commands would not notice. This is the
#                setting to change when moving to such a provider; nothing in
#                the code needs to change with it.
#
#   local        Off Redis entirely. An operator's lever for taking a limiter
#                out of a Redis incident without a deploy. Correct for one
#                instance, multiplies across several.
#
# An unrecognised value stops the boot rather than defaulting, because a typo
# here would silently change how strictly every volume limit is enforced.
# RATE_LIMIT_MODE=coordinated


# Per-IP ceiling underneath the per-address cap, so rotating the address cannot buy
# unlimited mail. Generous by design: it should only ever be hit by an
# address-enumeration attempt, never by a legitimate cohort.
# MAIL_IP_CEILING=120

# Failed sign-in attempts per 15 minutes, keyed on the DEVICE **and the account
# being tried** — not on the device alone. Successes are not counted.
#
# Per-IP-only was a school-wide lockout: ten different students each mistyping once
# exhausted the shared budget for the whole building, and the eleventh student —
# typing the CORRECT password — was refused with 429 for the rest of the window.
# Keying on the pair means one student's typos cannot spend another's allowance,
# while a single account still tolerates only this many failures from one device.
# The account-level counter in authRoutes.js (LOGIN_FAILURE_LIMIT, 10 per 15
# minutes, keyed on the account across ALL devices) is what bounds brute force.
# LOGIN_RATE_LIMIT=10
#
# Per-IP ceiling underneath the per-account limiter above: total FAILED sign-ins
# one address may accumulate across however many accounts, per 15 minutes. This is
# the anti-password-spray bound, and it is what makes per-account keying safe.
#
# Set above what a school generates and below what a spray needs. A
# two-thousand-student building on signup morning, a few percent mistyping once, is
# on the order of a hundred failures per quarter hour; a spray worth running wants
# thousands. Raise it for a larger population — but never so far that spraying
# becomes free, and never lower it to the point where a busy morning trips it.
# LOGIN_IP_CEILING=200

# The blanket limit on /api, per 15 minutes.
#
# Keyed on the SESSION when the request carries a valid one, and on the address
# otherwise — so one student's browsing no longer spends another's allowance.
# That matters here specifically: a school is one NAT address, and before this
# was keyed the whole building shared a single 600-request budget that a few
# dozen students at lunchtime exhausted.
#
# The per-bucket number is what to tune. A signed-in session makes several
# requests per screen, so 600 is roughly a few hundred screens in a quarter
# hour for ONE account. Raise it for a heavier population; there is no reason to
# lower it.
# API_RATE_LIMIT=600

# ── The other rate limits ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Listed because they exist, not because you should change them. Each is a
# security control with a measured default, and an operator who does not know a
# control exists cannot tune it, audit it, or explain it to a district — which
# is the only reason these are written down here rather than left in the code.
#
# All four read as `Number(process.env.X) || <default>`, so **0 does not mean
# zero** — it is falsy and silently restores the default. To block traffic, stop
# the endpoint at the proxy; do not try to express it here.
#
# Client error reports accepted per IP per 15 minutes, on the one unauthenticated
# write endpoint in the application (`POST /api/errors/report`). This bounds
# REQUESTS from one address and nothing else; the 500-group client ceiling in
# errorReporter.js is what bounds ROWS regardless of how many addresses the
# traffic comes from. Neither subsumes the other — see ENGINEERING_GUIDE §6.10.
# Raising it buys a noisier error table, not better diagnostics: a genuine bug is
# already one grouped row with a counter by the twentieth report.
# ERROR_REPORT_RATE_LIMIT=20
#
# Step-up re-authentication attempts per 15 minutes. This is the throttle on
# guessing a password at the AAL2 gate, so it is deliberately tight.
# REAUTH_RATE_LIMIT=5
#
# Data-export requests per 15 minutes. Each one assembles a complete copy of one
# account's records, so this bounds work as much as it bounds abuse.
# EXPORT_RATE_LIMIT=3

# How many distinct opportunities one student may have a PENDING application to
# at once. This is a business-state limit, not a rate limit: it bounds what an
# account holds, not how fast it asks. The unit is the opportunity, not the
# application row, so extra shifts of an event already applied to are free —
# applications are per shift, and counting rows would penalise a volunteer who
# commits to a whole weekend of one event. Only `pending` counts; an accepted
# place is a commitment rather than an outstanding request, a waitlisted one was
# done to the applicant by a draw, and a pending application on a cancelled
# event is not theirs to resolve. Raise it for a district whose students
# legitimately run more than ten decisions in flight.
# PENDING_APPLICATION_LIMIT=10

# How many signup requests one network address may make per 15 minutes, counting
# the ones that SUCCEED. Signup used to sit on the auth limiter, which skips
# successful requests, so account creation was bounded only by the blanket /api
# ceiling — about 2,400 an hour from one address, each sending a verification
# email to an address the caller chose. Raise it for a large district rolling out
# in one morning; a two-thousand-student building is on the order of a few
# hundred per quarter hour. Note this is a volume brake, not an identity control:
# a school shares one public address, so no per-IP figure separates an attacker
# from a campus. MAIL_RATE_LIMIT is what bounds attempts naming one address, and
# email verification is what leaves an unverified account inert.
# SIGNUP_RATE_LIMIT=300

# How fast one student may apply, per 15 minutes. The other half of the control
# above, and not redundant with it: withdrawal is free and leaves no record, so
# the quota alone puts no ceiling on how many applications a script can create
# and discard. Keyed on the hashed user id, NOT the address — a school NATs its
# whole campus, and an IP-keyed limit here would let one student lock the
# district out of signing up for anything.
# APPLICATION_RATE_LIMIT=30
#
# Consecutive failed sign-ins before an account is locked out. Keyed on the
# ACCOUNT, not the IP — a campus egresses from one address, so an IP-keyed lockout
# would let one student lock out the school.
# LOGIN_FAILURE_LIMIT=10
#
# ── Check-in codes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Both scan limits are keyed on the ACCOUNT, not the address. A campus egresses
# from one address, so an IP-keyed limit would let one organizer's rush stop
# every other organizer at the same school from checking anybody in.
#
# Scanning is a token bucket rather than a fixed window, because the traffic is
# a rush and not a stream: a hundred and fifty volunteers arrive in the ten
# minutes around the start and are scanned as fast as a camera can be pointed.
# A window sized for that is enormous for the rest of the day; a window sized
# for the rest of the day stops the organizer at the one moment the feature
# exists for, and the fallback is typing a hundred and fifty names.
#
# Burst capacity: scans one organizer may do back to back with no throttling.
# CHECKIN_SCAN_BURST_LIMIT=150
#
# Sustained scans per minute once the burst is spent. Enough for an all-day
# event, never enough for a machine.
# CHECKIN_SCAN_RATE_LIMIT=30
#
# Check-in codes a volunteer may mint per fifteen minutes. A code lasts five
# minutes, so an honest volunteer needs about three per shift plus retries.
# CHECKIN_CODE_RATE_LIMIT=20


# ── Organization invitation links and joining ────────────────────────────────
#
# An organization's invitation link is resolved at an UNAUTHENTICATED endpoint —
# somebody handed a link has no account, which is the whole point of the link —
# so, like the certificate check, it has no other guard against being used as a
# free database probe. Successes are counted deliberately: a successful lookup is
# exactly what walking a harvested list of links looks like from outside.
#
# The default is sized for the expected shape rather than for a lone visitor: a
# teacher puts a club's link on the board and thirty students open it in the same
# lesson, from one school address, inside one window. It bounds nothing about
# guessing — the token is 32 random bytes, so the search space is not reachable
# at any rate — so the number is set by the classroom, not by the attacker.
#
# Attempts to resolve an invitation link, per device, per fifteen minutes.
# ORG_INVITE_RATE_LIMIT=120
#
# Attempts to join or to ask to join an organization, per ACCOUNT, per fifteen
# minutes. This is the ONLY limit on the authenticated join route, and it is
# keyed on the account rather than the address for the reason every other limiter
# here gives: a school leaves the internet from one address, and a per-address
# budget would stop the students at the back of a class that is all joining the
# same club. Twenty is far above anything a person does — join three clubs,
# change their mind twice — and far below a client looping the endpoint with
# different tokens.
# ORG_JOIN_RATE_LIMIT=20


# ── Alerting ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# SET THIS. It is the only thing that causes anyone to look at the error
# dashboard. Without it the application records its own failures perfectly and
# tells nobody — which is how a pilot discovers a week-old outage from a student
# rather than from the software.
#
# Where to email when a NEW server or worker error appears. Comma-separated for
# more than one. Unset disables alerting entirely; that is the safe default, not
# a recommendation. Uses the SMTP_* settings above — no extra service to run.
#
# What the email contains: how many new problems, each one's error class, its
# fingerprint, and a link to /errors. What it deliberately does NOT contain:
# the error message, the stack trace, the route, or anything about who was
# affected. Those are personal data about minors and an inbox is not
# access-controlled; reading them requires signing in as the platform owner.
# `server/alerting.test.js` fails the build if any of it appears in the body.
#
# Client-side (browser) errors never alert. Their error class is supplied by
# whoever sent the report, and a browser extension throwing on someone's laptop
# is not an incident.
#
# ALERT_EMAIL=ops@district.example
#
# Minimum gap between two alert emails, in milliseconds. Default 3600000 (one
# hour). A cascade produces many distinct problems in seconds; without a floor
# that is one email each, which buries the first — the one that mattered — and
# teaches the recipient to filter the channel. Nothing is dropped by waiting: a
# throttled problem is included in the next digest, because eligibility is "has
# this been alerted on" and not a time window.
# ALERT_MIN_INTERVAL_MS=3600000
#
# ── Backup failures alert separately, through systemd ────────────────────────
#
# Nothing here covers a failed backup: `bin/backup.sh` exits non-zero and systemd
# records a failed unit, but a failed unit nobody looks at is the same as no
# backup. Add an `OnFailure=` to deploy/wevolunteer-backup.service — the runbook
# has the unit. This is a separate mechanism on purpose: a backup job that cannot
# run is exactly the situation where the application may not be running either,
# so routing that alert through the application would be routing it through the
# thing that is broken.

# ── Provider sign-in (optional; email-only is a supported deployment) ────────
#
# Set all three together, or none of them. With client credentials set, the
# server REFUSES TO START unless OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI names this deployment —
# because its built-in default is the development value
# `http://localhost:5173/oauth/callback`, and shipping that would send every user
# who presses "Continue with Google" to their own machine (and, if localhost is
# still registered on the OAuth client, deliver a live authorization code there
# over plain HTTP).
#
# OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI must match a redirect URI registered with the provider
# EXACTLY — scheme, host, port, path. It must be https outside localhost; both
# Google and Microsoft refuse anything else.
#
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=REPLACE_ME.apps.googleusercontent.com
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=REPLACE_ME
# OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=https://app.wevolunteers.org/oauth/callback
#
# `/oauth/callback` is an SPA route served by the application origin, so under
# the domain split it is the `app.` host — register exactly this string in the
# Google Cloud console and in the Entra App Registration.
#
# Microsoft (Entra ID / Microsoft identity platform). Same shape as Google, plus
# an optional tenant restriction. Requires an App Registration in the Azure
# portal — Redirect URI type "Web", set to exactly the OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI above,
# and a client secret from Certificates & secrets. The registration must include
# the delegated `openid`, `profile` and `email` permissions; nothing more is
# requested, and `User.Read` is deliberately NOT needed (the identity comes from
# the ID token, not from a Graph lookup).
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=REPLACE_ME
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=REPLACE_ME
#
# Which directory may sign in. Unset means `common`: ANY Entra tenant and any
# personal Microsoft account on the internet can complete the flow. That is the
# right default for a district that has not decided, and the wrong one for a
# district that has — set the tenant GUID (Overview → Directory (tenant) ID) and
# every other directory is refused, both at the provider and again on the `tid`
# claim when the token comes back.
# MICROSOFT_TENANT_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
#
# Apple has no implementation behind it and is always reported as unavailable,
# whatever is set here.
#
# NEVER set OAUTH_ALLOW_MOCK in this file. It replaces the provider with an
# identity read straight out of the request body — anyone could sign in as
# anyone — and a production process that finds it set to "true" refuses to boot
# rather than starting insecure. It exists for automated tests only.

# Total Auto Select draws permitted per event.
# MAX_AUTO_SELECT_DRAWS=5

# Require a confirmed email address before an account can be used.
# Only turn this on once mail delivery is proven — without it, new users are
# locked out with no way through.
# REQUIRE_EMAIL_VERIFICATION=true


# ── Single-instance enforcement ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Exactly ONE instance may run against a database. This is now enforced: a second
# production instance whose predecessor is still heartbeating refuses to boot and
# names the holder. It is a data-integrity control — all rate-limit state is
# per-process memory, and slot-capacity checks read outside their write transaction.
#
# A crashed predecessor never blocks a restart: a dead pid on the same host is
# reclaimed immediately, and a stale heartbeat is reclaimed after this window.
# INSTANCE_LOCK_STALE_MS=45000
# INSTANCE_LOCK_HEARTBEAT_MS=15000

# ── Node runtime ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The server refuses to start on any Node major other than 24.x, because the whole
# data layer is `node:sqlite`, which Node still marks EXPERIMENTAL — its API may
# change between majors, and the failure mode is silent data damage rather than a
# clean error. `.nvmrc` records the exact tested version.
#
# Only set this if you have deliberately validated another major yourself.
# WEV_ALLOW_UNSUPPORTED_NODE=true

# ── Never set here ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# DEMO_MODE seeds accounts sharing a published password (including an admin) and
# raises the rate limits roughly 100x. The server refuses to start if it is
# combined with NODE_ENV=production, but do not add it at all.
#
# It no longer registers any database-reset endpoint: that route was removed
# outright, because it destroyed the database instead of resetting it. Restoring
# a development database is now `npm run db:reset:dev`, which refuses to run when
# NODE_ENV=production.

# ── Travel times (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# An OpenRouteService API key, used by /api/travel-time to answer "how long is
# the drive to this shift". Sign up at https://openrouteservice.org/dev/#/signup
#
# Leave it unset and the feature turns itself off silently: the route answers
# 503 routing_unconfigured, the page renders no travel time, and the "Get
# directions" link — which needs no provider at all — carries on working. That
# is a supported state, not a broken one.
#
# The key is read only in the server process and is never sent to the browser.
# A key in a browser bundle is a published key.
#
# What the provider receives: a pair of coordinates, coarsened to three decimal
# places (~110 m) before the request leaves this process, in a POST body rather
# than a query string. Nothing identifies the student, and no origin is ever
# written to the database or to a log.
#
# ROUTING_API_KEY=

# How many travel-time requests one ACCOUNT may make per 15 minutes.
# Keyed on a hash of the user id, never on the address: a school NATs its whole
# campus behind one IP, and an IP-keyed limiter once locked out a building.
# TRAVEL_TIME_RATE_LIMIT=60

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Who the public About page names as running this deployment.
#
# The username of the account to feature. It must already hold `super_admin` —
# this only chooses BETWEEN owners and cannot create one, so pointing it at a
# student resolves to nothing and the page falls back to the earliest owner.
#
# Worth setting whenever an install has more than one owner account. This one
# has two — a person and a service account, seeded in the same run with
# identical timestamps — and without this the page features whichever id sorted
# first, which is nobody's decision.
#
# Unset is fine: the earliest active owner is named instead.
# PLATFORM_OWNER_USERNAME=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Operational knobs with sensible defaults.
#
# All of these are read by the server and none has to be set — the default in
# brackets is what runs if you leave it alone. They are listed because a value
# that exists in code and nowhere in this file is a value nobody knows they can
# change, and because `envDocumentation.test.js` only scans names ending in
# _RATE_LIMIT, _LIMIT or _CEILING, so the rest never had a guard.

# Where the backup archives live, and how old the newest one may be before the
# app calls it stale on the dashboard and emails ALERT_EMAIL about it.
# The timer runs nightly with up to 5 minutes of jitter, so 26 hours catches a
# single missed night without firing on a slow run. [26]
# WEV_BACKUP_DIR=/var/backups/wevolunteer
# BACKUP_STALE_HOURS=26

# Public key the nightly backup encrypts to. See §9 of PRE-RELEASE.md — the
# private half must NOT be on this host.
# WEV_BACKUP_PUBKEY=/etc/wevolunteer/backup-cert.pem

# How often the /health endpoint attempts a real page WRITE, in milliseconds.
# A read succeeds on a full disk and a read-only mount, so the write probe is
# what makes the check meaningful; 60s keeps the WAL churn down. [60000]
# HEALTH_WRITE_PROBE_MS=60000

# Access log. One line per request: method, path (query string stripped),
# status, duration, and the user id. Defaults to ON in production.
# REQUEST_LOG=true

# How long a graceful shutdown waits for in-flight work before exiting. [10000]
# SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# Auto Select. The scheduler can be disabled outright, and the grace period is
# how long after an application deadline a draw may still run. [24]
# AUTO_SELECT_SCHEDULER=false
# AUTO_SELECT_GRACE_HOURS=24
# How often the scheduler wakes to look for draws that are due. [900000 = 15 min]
# AUTO_SELECT_INTERVAL_MS=900000

# How long a data-export archive survives before the worker deletes it, and how
# long a queued notification may sit before it is considered stuck. Milliseconds.
# EXPORT_STALE_MS=604800000
# NOTIFICATION_STALE_MS=3600000

# Reports about PEOPLE (the moderation queue), per reporter per 15 minutes. [5]
# REPORT_RATE_LIMIT=5

# The API port, when the client and server are served separately in development.
# In production one process serves both and this is not used. [5000]
# API_PORT=5000

# Overrides the Resend endpoint. Exists so a test can point at a local stub;
# there is no reason to set it in a real deployment.
# RESEND_API_URL=https://api.resend.com/emails

# DEMO_MODE relaxes rate limits and seeds demo accounts. Production REFUSES TO
# BOOT with it set — it is listed here so nobody adds it looking for a fix.
# DEMO_MODE=true

# EMAIL_FROM is read and IGNORED. The sender is chosen per message by
# server/emailIdentity.js; set MAIL_DOMAIN instead. The server says so at boot.

# ── Database engine ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `sqlite` (the default) or `postgres`. Both are supported and boot-tested.
# SQLite is what this deployment currently runs on; the cutover procedure,
# its rollback, and what each step costs are in
# docs/operations/postgres-cutover-and-rollback.md.
#
# `POSTGRES_READY` is true as of 2026-08-18: the adapter is finished, the
# async port is complete (4,800+ tests pass against a real PostgreSQL server),
# and the production boot has been rehearsed. The default remains `sqlite`
# until an operator deliberately sets `DATABASE_ENGINE=postgres` and provides
# a `DATABASE_URL` — nothing changes automatically.
#
# A typo is refused rather than defaulted: `DATABASE_ENGINE=postgre` stops
# the boot instead of quietly opening SQLite, because a silent fallback
# looks exactly like a successful migration.
# DATABASE_ENGINE=sqlite

# ── Realtime across multiple backend instances ────────────────────────────────
#
# Socket.IO rooms live in the process that owns the socket. With ONE instance —
# which `instanceLock.js` enforces, and which is what this deployment runs —
# that is invisible and correct. With two and no adapter, a broadcast reaches
# only the clients attached to the instance that handled the write, so
# notifications, messages and read receipts are delivered to roughly half the
# connected users, silently.
#
# Leave REALTIME_ADAPTER unset unless you are actually running more than one
# backend instance. Unset is the deployed state and the package is not even
# loaded.
#
# When you do: `postgres` is the only supported value. The adapter broadcasts
# over LISTEN/NOTIFY, so the bus is the database that is already here — no new
# service, no new credential, no new free-tier ceiling.
# REALTIME_ADAPTER=postgres

# The adapter's OWN connection string, and it must not go through a
# transaction-mode pooler.
#
# LISTEN holds state on a connection between statements. Supavisor in
# TRANSACTION mode (port 6543) returns the connection to the pool after every
# transaction, so the LISTEN is simply gone — no error, no notifications, and a
# cluster that looks healthy while broadcasting nothing.
#
# DATABASE_URL above is meant to point at exactly that pooler, which is right
# for the application's own queries. So this is deliberately NOT defaulted to
# it: inheriting it would be correct on a direct connection and broken on the
# pooler the runbook recommends. Setting REALTIME_ADAPTER without this stops the
# boot.
#
# Use the SESSION-mode pooler (port 5432 on the pooler host) or a direct
# connection.
# REALTIME_ADAPTER_URL=postgres://user:password@aws-0-region.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres


# The PostgreSQL connection string, when that engine is selected. It contains the
# database password, so it belongs in the systemd EnvironmentFile and NEVER in a
# VITE_-prefixed variable — those are compiled into the browser bundle.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host:5432/wevolunteer

# Client-side pool size, PER PROCESS. [25]
#
# ── Measured, not reasoned ──
#
# This was 10, on the argument that PostgreSQL gains nothing from more
# concurrent connections than it has cores. That is true of CPU-bound queries
# and it is the wrong model over a network: a pooled client there spends most of
# its life waiting on a round trip, so the pool has to be sized for requests in
# flight rather than for cores.
#
# Measured with bin/load-test.mjs at a 3 ms round trip and 20 concurrent
# requests (--latency-profile same-region --soak 45 --pool-max N):
#
#     pool    req/s     p50      p95      p99   peak waiting
#        5     67.7   203ms   1027ms   1164ms       19
#       10    127.2   120ms    481ms    526ms       17
#       15    269.2    56ms    161ms    194ms       13
#       20    403.4    37ms     97ms    106ms        9
#       25    486.8    29ms     73ms     80ms        5
#       40    501.8    24ms     58ms     64ms        0
#
# 25 takes ~97% of the achievable throughput and drops p95 by 6.6x against the
# old default; past it the curve is flat. The same sweep with NO injected
# latency is noise (863 / 1011 / 706 / 871 req/s at 10 / 20 / 30 / 50), because
# loopback makes the application's own event loop the bottleneck instead — which
# is why the old number looked defensible for as long as it did.
#
# A non-zero `waiting` is NOT by itself a problem: a small pool queues by
# design, and the healthy loopback runs above show waiting of 7-10 with zero
# errors. It is the combination of sustained waiting AND climbing p95 that says
# the pool is the constraint.
#
# ── Which host and port to point DATABASE_URL at, on Supabase ──
#
# Supabase gives three connection strings, and they differ by HOST as well as
# port. Checked against Supabase's compute-and-disk documentation, 2026-08-30,
# for the Nano (free) and Micro sizes:
#
#   db.<ref>.supabase.co:5432                  direct.  60 connections
#   aws-<n>-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432  Supavisor, SESSION mode.     ] 200
#   aws-<n>-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543  Supavisor, TRANSACTION mode. ] clients
#
# Use the pooler in TRANSACTION mode (6543). The application is already built for
# it: `server/db/postgresConnection.js` leaves `pg`'s named prepared statements
# OFF precisely because a transaction-mode pooler can put a different backend
# under a client between checkouts, and a named statement prepared on one backend
# does not exist on another. Unnamed statements are re-planned each time and are
# correct everywhere.
#
# Session mode works too and buys nothing here: this application holds no session
# state in a connection — no SET, no advisory locks, no LISTEN/NOTIFY, and no
# cursor that outlives a statement — so it gains nothing from a connection that
# is pinned for the session, while giving up the larger client budget.
#
# Direct (5432) is the one to avoid for the application. Keep it for migrations,
# pg_dump and psql, where a stable session genuinely matters.
#
# ── The direct host is IPv6-ONLY, and the pooler is IPv4-only ──
#
# Not a preference — an address-family fact, checked by DNS on 2026-09-02
# against a live Supabase project and against Supabase's own connection
# documentation:
#
#   db.<ref>.supabase.co                 AAAA only, no A record
#   aws-<n>-<region>.pooler.supabase.com A records only
#
# Supabase's docs say the same in words: the direct endpoint is on IPv6, "or on
# IPv4 if the project has the IPv4 add-on", which is a paid extra. So on an
# IPv4-only host the direct connection string does not merely perform worse, it
# cannot connect at all — and the failure is a DNS/connect error that looks
# nothing like a credential problem.
#
# Two consequences worth knowing before an incident:
#
#   1. The advice below to keep the direct host "for migrations, pg_dump and
#      psql" assumes the machine running them has IPv6. Check with
#      `dig AAAA db.<ref>.supabase.co` and `curl -6 -sS https://…` from THAT
#      machine, not from a laptop.
#   2. If it does not, run those against the pooler in SESSION mode (5432),
#      which is IPv4 and holds a connection for the session. Transaction mode
#      (6543) is the wrong one for pg_dump and for anything that expects a
#      stable session.
#
# ── The arithmetic ──
#
# The number that matters is pool size x processes, and it must stay under the
# ceiling with room for the operator's own psql session and for the backup job:
#
#   1 instance  x 25 =  25   comfortable against the pooler's 200
#   2 instances x 25 =  50   comfortable
#   8 instances x 25 = 200   at the POOLER ceiling
#   2 instances x 25 =  50   already past the DIRECT ceiling of 60 at three
#                            instances — another reason to use the pooler
#
# Raising DATABASE_POOL_MAX means redoing this arithmetic. It is per process, so
# the number that matters is pool x instances, and it must leave room for the
# operator's own psql session and the backup job.
#
#
# Turn HEALTH_POOL_STATS on to watch `waiting` if you suspect otherwise.
# A rising `waiting` means requests are queueing for a client, which looks
# exactly like a slow database from the outside and is not the same thing.
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=25

# Statement and connection timeouts are NOT set here, deliberately.
#
# `pg` is given `connectionTimeoutMillis: 10000` and `idleTimeoutMillis: 30000`
# in `server/db/postgresConnection.js`. A query timeout is not, because the only
# safe place to abandon a statement is one where abandoning it cannot leave a
# half-finished write — and this application's writes run inside transactions
# whose rollback is the thing that makes them safe. A per-statement timeout added
# without that analysis converts a slow query into a partially-applied change.
#
# If long-running statements become a problem, set `statement_timeout` on the
# database role instead, where PostgreSQL enforces it with the transaction
# semantics intact.

# TLS is ON by default for a remote host and OFF for localhost. Set `true` to
# force it on locally, `false` to turn it off for a remote host — which sends the
# password and every student record in clear text, so do not.
# DATABASE_SSL=true

# Skips certificate verification. Only for a provider using a self-signed cert,
# and never as a way to make a TLS error go away.
# DATABASE_SSL_INSECURE=false

# Set ONLY when running the test suite, to exercise the PostgreSQL adapter tests
# (transaction pinning, pool isolation, adapter semantics). Unset, those tests
# report as skipped rather than passing vacuously. Not read by the server.
# TEST_POSTGRES_URL=postgres://postgres@127.0.0.1:5432/wevolunteer_test

# ── Better Stack telemetry (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# With these unset the service logs to the systemd journal exactly as before and
# sends nothing anywhere. Better Stack is never a boot requirement and never a
# dependency for serving a request: if the endpoint is unreachable the batch is
# dropped, one warning is written to the journal, and the application carries on.
#
# See docs/operations/betterstack.md for how to create the source, what is sent,
# and what is deliberately never sent.
#
# BETTERSTACK_SOURCE_TOKEN is a write credential for your log stream. It belongs
# in this file (mode 0600, root-owned, /etc/wevolunteer/wevolunteer.env) and
# nowhere else — never in the repository, never in the frontend .env.production.
# BETTERSTACK_SOURCE_TOKEN=
# BETTERSTACK_INGESTING_HOST=

# Fraction of SUCCESSFUL requests logged (0–1). Errors, 4xx/5xx refusals and
# requests slower than one second are always logged regardless.
# TELEMETRY_SAMPLE_RATE=0.05

# Distinguishes deployments that share one Better Stack account.
# TELEMETRY_SERVICE_NAME=wevolunteer

# Overrides the `release` field, which otherwise comes from server/package.json.
# APP_RELEASE=

# ── Map tiles (optional key) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Event maps are drawn from tiles this server fetches and relays at /tiles, so
# a student's browser never contacts a map provider (server/routes/tileRoutes.js).
#
# Unset: OpenStreetMap's standard tiles, which need no key. Dark mode dims them.
#
# Set: CARTO's Voyager and dark_all basemaps, the styles the product was designed
# with. CARTO requires a key for them now — without one every tile carries
# "API KEY REQUIRED" — and offers a free non-commercial key (5 million tiles a
# month) at https://carto.com/basemaps/apikey. Restrict it to this server.
#
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY, and never VITE_: a key in the bundle is a key anyone can
# copy with View Source. The proxy is what keeps it here.
#
# MAP_TILES_CARTO_KEY=REPLACE_ME

# ── Pinecone: semantic search for Discover (optional) ────────────────────────
#
# Off unless PINECONE_API_KEY is set. With it unset, Discover uses the database
# substring search it has always used — a complete answer, not a degraded one.
#
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY. Never prefix any of these with VITE_: that would compile the
# key into the browser bundle, where it is a write credential for your index
# that anyone can read with View Source.
#
# PINECONE_API_KEY=pcsk_...
#
# The index name carries its schema version. Bumping the version means a new
# index and a fresh backfill; see docs/operations/pinecone.md.
# PINECONE_INDEX=wevolunteer-discover-v1
#
# Namespaces separate deployments inside ONE index. Defaults to NODE_ENV, so a
# developer who sets only the API key cannot write test events into production.
# Starter allows 100 namespaces per index and only 5 indexes, so namespaces are
# the right axis to separate on.
#
# Leave it UNSET on the production host: it then resolves to `production`. The
# value below is the development example — uncommented here, it would point the
# live Discover search at the development partition of the same index. That is
# not hypothetical: a production boot from a developer's environment file came
# up on `development` for exactly this reason (docs/audits/2026-09-09-…, §19).
# PINECONE_NAMESPACE=development
#
# Starter indexes MUST be in us-east-1 on AWS. These exist for a later plan.
# PINECONE_CLOUD=aws
# PINECONE_REGION=us-east-1
#
# Skips one control-plane round trip per boot. Optional — the host is resolved
# and remembered automatically.
# PINECONE_INDEX_HOST=
#
# Reranking is OPT-IN. The Starter plan allows 500 rerank requests per MONTH,
# about sixteen a day, so this is a budget decision rather than a quality one.
# Leave it off unless you have measured your search volume.
# PINECONE_RERANK=0
# PINECONE_RERANK_BUDGET=400
#
# How long Discover waits for the semantic half before answering with the
# database results alone.
# PINECONE_SEARCH_TIMEOUT_MS=1200
#
# How often the background worker drains the index queue.
# PINECONE_SYNC_INTERVAL_MS=15000

# ============================================
# Upstash Redis (optional)
# ============================================

# Off unless BOTH variables below are set. With them unset the application
# behaves exactly as it always has: every rate limiter counts in process memory
# and every third-party lookup cache lives in this process and dies with it.
# Redis is NEVER required to serve a request.
#
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY. Never prefix either of these with VITE_: that would compile
# a full-privilege database token into the browser bundle, where anyone can read
# it with View Source.
#
# What it is used for, and deliberately not used for:
#   USED   the account lockout (ten failed passwords per account) plus the mail,
#          re-authentication, appeal and certificate-check limiters, so a
#          restart no longer clears an in-progress lockout;
#   NOT    the three per-device sign-in limiters. They set
#          skipSuccessfulRequests, so they bill on successful sign-ins too, and
#          sharing them costs more than the whole rest of this integration
#          combined — 107% of the monthly allowance at 1,000 users. See
#          docs/operations/upstash.md §4.
#   USED   caching weather and address lookups, so a deploy does not re-warm
#          them against somebody else's usage policy;
#   NOT    sessions, chat, the leaderboard, or ANY database query. The hot
#          queries here are 0.3-0.5 ms and an Upstash round trip is far slower,
#          so caching them would be a regression that also costs quota.
# Full reasoning: docs/operations/upstash.md
#
# Create a database in the Upstash console (https://console.upstash.com):
# choose REGIONAL, not global — a global database replicates writes to read
# regions and counts each replication as a command, doubling the spend for a
# single-server deployment. Pick the region nearest this host. The console's
# "REST API" tab gives you both values below.
#
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN is a WRITE credential for the whole database. Treat
# it like a password: never commit it, never put it in a VITE_ variable, and
# rotate it in the Upstash console if it leaks.
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=https://your-db-name.upstash.io
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=your-rest-token-here
#
# The free plan allows ONE database, so development, test and production share a
# keyspace and the prefix is the only thing separating them. It defaults to
# NODE_ENV, so a developer who sets only the credentials cannot write test
# counters into production's. Set this only when two deployments share a
# NODE_ENV.
# UPSTASH_KEY_PREFIX=production
#
# How long one Upstash call may take before the caller gives up and uses its
# fallback. Short on purpose: every caller has a correct answer without Redis,
# so waiting is worse than falling back.
# UPSTASH_TIMEOUT_MS=800
#
# How long the circuit breaker stays open after a failure. Without it an outage
# costs one timeout on every request that would have used Redis, which turns a
# degraded dependency into a degraded sign-in page.
# UPSTASH_BREAKER_MS=30000

# In production this file is the only place the key belongs: mode 0600,
# root-owned, /etc/wevolunteer/wevolunteer.env. Never in the repository and
# never in the frontend .env.production.

# ── OpenTelemetry: traces and metrics ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Uses BETTERSTACK_SOURCE_TOKEN and BETTERSTACK_INGESTING_HOST above; there is
# no second credential. Both default sensibly and neither needs setting.
#
# OTEL_TRACE_SAMPLE_RATE is a COST control: the Better Stack free plan includes
# 3 GB of traces a month and bills beyond it. Raising it past a few percent is
# how this deployment starts costing money. OTEL_TRACE_DAILY_SPAN_BUDGET is the
# hard ceiling underneath it; reaching it turns trace sampling off until the
# window rolls and changes nothing else. Full reasoning and the budget table:
# docs/operations/observability.md
#
# OTEL_TRACE_SAMPLE_RATE=0.02
# OTEL_TRACE_DAILY_SPAN_BUDGET=200000
# WEV_OTEL_ENABLED=false

# ── The Owner Center (/owner) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The owner-only operations page. It works with none of these set — the part that
# matters most on a bad day (this process, this database, the error trend, mail
# and queue health) is read locally and contacts nothing. Each variable switches
# on one more card; without it the card says "Not configured" and names the
# variable. Full setup and the free-plan position of each integration:
# docs/operations/owner-center.md
#
# A FOURTH Better Stack credential, and not interchangeable with the source token
# above: Better Stack issues a separate token per surface. This one reads uptime
# monitors and incidents, and nothing here acknowledges, resolves or creates
# anything. Better Stack -> API tokens -> Team-based tokens (Uptime).
# BETTERSTACK_UPTIME_API_TOKEN=
#
# Amplitude usage figures. NOT the same key as the frontend's
# VITE_AMPLITUDE_API_KEY, which is public by design and is a write key for
# ingestion — this pair can READ every event in the project, so it belongs in
# this file (mode 0600, root-owned) and must never be given a VITE_ prefix.
# AMPLITUDE_API_KEY=
# AMPLITUDE_SECRET_KEY=
# AMPLITUDE_REGION=us
#
# How long a third party has to answer before its card reads "Took too long to
# answer".
# OWNER_PROVIDER_TIMEOUT_MS=6000
